#: src/features/Blog/blogData.tsx:53
#. js-lingui-explicit-id
msgid ""
"Naively, I assumed the book would give me tools - techniques for decoding "
"people, keys to reading intentions, perhaps even a method for understanding "
"the stranger in my own home. But the book is really anything but.\n"
"\n"
"It argues that our desire to understand others is so strong that we "
"convince ourselves we already do. We take fragments - tone of voice, "
"posture, reputation, context - and from them construct entire "
"personalities. When our guesses happen to be correct, we treat that as "
"proof of our skill. When they fail, we call it an exception.\n"
"\n"
"And so our confidence grows.\n"
"\n"
"But who is a “stranger”? Not just the person in the elevator or the cashier "
"at the store. A stranger can be a colleague you’ve worked with for years. A "
"friend who has shared secrets. Even someone who has quietly entered your "
"everyday life and begun to shape it.\n"
"\n"
"We learn a few details. We hear a few confessions. We observe a few "
"patterns. Then we exhale, believing we have understood. What we rarely "
"notice is how many blanks we have filled in ourselves - motives, "
"intentions, inner states.\n"
"\n"
"We can mistake coherence for truth.\n"
"\n"
"The book contains unsettling examples. One of them centers on a respected "
"figure in the financial world - a man with status, reputation, elite "
"connections, institutional credibility. For decades, investors trusted him. "
"Major players invested. Returns appeared stable. Everything about him "
"signaled reliability.\n"
"\n"
"Behind that facade stood one of the largest financial frauds in history: "
"the scheme of Bernie Madoff.\n"
"\n"
"It seems that his operation lasted so long because people felt safe. "
"Reputation became a substitute for verification. Social proof handled "
"skepticism. When millions trust someone, doubt begins to feel almost "
"irrational - even embarrassing.\n"
"\n"
"It becomes easier to assume that someone else has already asked the hard "
"questions."
msgstr ""
"Dengan naifnya, saya menyangka buku itu akan memberi saya alat - teknik "
"untuk memecahkan kod orang, kunci untuk membaca niat, mungkin juga kaedah "
"untuk memahami orang asing di rumah saya sendiri. Tetapi buku ini "
"sebenarnya bukan seperti itu.\n"
"\n"
"Ia berhujah bahawa keinginan kita untuk memahami orang lain begitu kuat "
"sehingga kita meyakinkan diri kita bahawa kita sudah melakukannya. Kita "
"mengambil serpihan - nada suara, postur, reputasi, konteks - dan "
"daripadanya membina keseluruhan peribadi. Apabila tekaan kita kebetulan "
"tepat, kita menganggap itu sebagai bukti kemahiran kita. Apabila gagal, "
"kita menyebutnya sebagai pengecualian.\n"
"\n"
"Dan dengan itu keyakinan kita semakin meningkat.\n"
"\n"
"Tetapi siapa “orang asing”? Bukan hanya orang dalam lif atau juruwang di "
"kedai. Orang asing boleh menjadi rakan sekerja yang telah anda bekerja "
"bersama bertahun-tahun. Seorang kawan yang telah berkongsi rahsia. Malah "
"seseorang yang senyap-senyap memasuki kehidupan harian anda dan mula "
"membentuknya.\n"
"\n"
"Kita mempelajari beberapa butiran. Kita mendengar beberapa pengakuan. Kita "
"memerhatikan beberapa pola. Kemudian kita menghela nafas, percaya bahawa "
"kita telah memahami. Apa yang jarang kita perhatikan ialah betapa banyak "
"tempat kosong yang telah kita isi sendiri - motif, niat, keadaan dalam.\n"
"\n"
"Kita boleh tersilap anggap koheren sebagai kebenaran.\n"
"\n"
"Buku ini mengandungi contoh-contoh yang mengganggu. Salah satunya berpusar "
"pada seorang tokoh yang dihormati dalam dunia kewangan - seorang lelaki "
"dengan status, reputasi, hubungan elit, kredibiliti institusi. Selama "
"beberapa dekad, pelabur mempercayainya. Pemain utama melabur. Pulangan "
"kelihatan stabil. Segala-galanya mengenainya memberi isyarat "
"kebolehpercayaan.\n"
"\n"
"Di sebalik facade itu berdiri salah satu penipuan kewangan terbesar dalam "
"sejarah: skim Bernie Madoff.\n"
"\n"
"Nampaknya operasi beliau bertahan begitu lama kerana orang merasa selamat. "
"Reputasi menjadi pengganti kepada pengesahan. Bukti sosial menangani "
"skeptisisme. Apabila berjuta-juta mempercayai seseorang, keraguan mula "
"terasa hampir tidak rasional - bahkan memalukan.\n"
"\n"
"Ia menjadi lebih mudah untuk menganggap bahawa orang lain sudahpun "
"menanyakan soalan-soalan yang sukar."

